# =============================================================================
# ui/.env.example
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Purpose:
#   Value-free template of every environment variable the CardDemo single-page
#   application reads. Copy it to ui/.env, fill in the values for whichever
#   environment you are pointing at, and keep that copy local: the copy is
#   untracked, this template is tracked, and that asymmetry is the point of the
#   file.
#
#   Each entry below records the three things a bare name cannot -- what
#   consumes the variable, where a real value originates, and what goes wrong
#   when it is absent. No entry carries a value.
#
# WHY (non-obvious design decisions):
#   - Assumptions: the VITE_ prefix is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. Vite
#     exposes ONLY variables matching its `envPrefix` to client code through
#     `import.meta.env` and withholds everything else from the bundle, so the
#     prefix is the mechanical boundary between configuration that may ship to a
#     browser and values that must never leave the server. That is why every
#     name here carries it, and why ui/vite.config.ts must set `envPrefix` to
#     the same string. A name added here WITHOUT the prefix would not fail
#     loudly -- it would simply be invisible at run time.
#   - Assumptions: whatever is supplied for these names is compiled INTO the
#     public JavaScript bundle and is readable by anyone who loads the page.
#     They therefore suit non-secret, publicly discoverable configuration only.
#     This is why no credential can legitimately appear here even in a
#     developer's own ui/.env: the browser obtains its bearer token by posting
#     the sign-on screen's credential to the auth service at run time, so no
#     password, client secret or access key belongs in this file or in any copy
#     of it, in any form. See the Identity section below, which declares an
#     absence rather than a variable, and which states exactly where the token
#     the browser receives is then held -- in MEMORY, written to no store at all.
#     That is recorded there rather than here because it is a property of the
#     session and not of this file. ⚠️ Two earlier revisions of this paragraph
#     each named the wrong one: the first said "memory" when the session was in
#     tab-scoped sessionStorage, and the second said sessionStorage after a
#     review moved it into memory. Either way round the mistake costs a reader
#     two false conclusions -- whether a reload ends the session, and whether a
#     script on this origin can read a credential -- so the Identity section is
#     the single place that answers both, and this paragraph only points at it.
#   - Assumptions: "compiled into" fixes WHEN these values are read, and it is at
#     BUILD time, not at run time. Vite inlines each one into the emitted
#     JavaScript as a literal while `vite build` runs, so the artifact is specific
#     to the environment whose variables were present at that moment. Two
#     consequences follow that a reader would otherwise have to discover. Each
#     environment needs its own build and its own image: there is no later
#     substitution step, and setting one of these names on a running container
#     changes nothing the browser can see, because the browser never reads the
#     container's environment. And a value supplied only at deploy time arrives
#     too late to have any effect at all -- the bundle already holds whatever was
#     inlined, so the symptom is a SPA addressing the wrong API with no error
#     anywhere to explain why. ui/nginx.conf is written on the same premise: it
#     holds nothing environment-specific because the bundle it serves already
#     does.
#   - Trade-offs: every right-hand side is EMPTY, and in particular there is no
#     localhost default. A committed default would appear to BE set, so an
#     environment that never supplied the real value would fail later and
#     further away -- as a connection error against a host that does not exist
#     there -- instead of failing at once as absent configuration. The cost
#     accepted is that a new developer must populate ui/.env before the SPA can
#     reach an API: a one-time, self-evident step, traded for a misconfiguration
#     that cannot hide.
#   - Assumptions: none of these values is authored by hand. They originate as
#     Terraform outputs recorded in SSM Parameter Store and Secrets Manager at
#     apply time and are read from there per environment, so no endpoint is
#     hard-coded in the SPA or in a service and no secret reaches a tfvars file.
#     That provenance is what makes "no secrets in the repository" a structural
#     property of the deployment rather than a habit of its reviewers.
#   - Alternatives Considered: a VITE_ENV or VITE_STAGE name was evaluated and
#     rejected. Vite already exposes the build mode as `import.meta.env.MODE`,
#     with the `DEV` and `PROD` booleans derived from it, so a hand-maintained
#     copy would be a second source of truth able to disagree with the mode the
#     bundle was actually built in -- and that disagreement surfaces as
#     behaviour contradicting the build, which is harder to diagnose than having
#     no such variable at all.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API access
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Prefixed onto every request by the shared axios instance in
# ui/src/api/client.ts. It addresses the API Gateway HTTP API that fronts the
# internal load balancer, which is the only way in: the SPA never reaches a
# service or a database directly.
# Assumptions: supplied per environment from the API Gateway endpoint Terraform
# emits as an output. Left empty per the header trade-off -- and note what an
# unset value actually does: axios then resolves every call against the origin
# serving the SPA, which answers with the SPA's own HTML instead of JSON, so the
# failure reads as a response-parsing bug rather than as missing configuration.
# That misdirection is precisely why this is never defaulted.
# WHY : Assumptions: this variable is the LOCAL-DEVELOPMENT source of the API base URL only. Vite
#       inlines it when the bundle is built, so it cannot carry a deployed environment's endpoint --
#       that endpoint does not exist until the infrastructure apply that creates it, which runs after
#       the build. A deployed environment is configured instead by a `config.json` document published
#       beside the bundle and read at start-up; see ui/src/api/runtimeConfig.ts. The published
#       document wins wherever it exists, and this value is the fallback.
#       Assumptions: the value must include the /api/v1 prefix, because operations are addressed
#       relatively by the client and the gateway publishes its routes under that prefix.
#       For example: http://localhost:8080/api/v1
VITE_API_BASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity -- deliberately no variable at all
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This section declares an ABSENCE, and it is the most consequential entry in
# the file. The SPA holds NO identity configuration: no issuer, no user-pool
# identifier, no app-client identifier, no region, and above all no client
# secret. It never calls the identity provider. It posts the user id and
# password the sign-on screen collects to the auth service's own endpoint,
# receives a bearer token, and thereafter sends that token with every request.
# ui/src/hooks/useAuth.ts holds the token, the group list decoded from it, and
# sign-out, which is likewise an auth-service endpoint rather than a call to the
# pool.
#
# Where the session is actually held, and for how long
# ----------------------------------------------------
# Assumptions: the session lives in MEMORY and is written nowhere. There is no
# `sessionStorage` key, no `localStorage` key, no cookie and no IndexedDB record
# to name, clear or audit. It consists of one frozen record inside
# ui/src/hooks/useAuth.ts -- the identifier, the identity token, the renewal
# token and the bearer's expiry instant -- plus the bearer itself in one module
# variable inside ui/src/api/client.ts, reachable only through that module's own
# `setAccessToken`. Nothing published by either module returns a credential.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: ⚠️ this section previously named FOUR session-storage
# keys -- carddemo.access-token, carddemo.id-token, carddemo.user-id and
# carddemo.refresh-token -- and argued for them. A review established that as an
# exposure rather than a trade-off: every credential the application held,
# including a renewal token provisioned with a thirty-day life
# (`refresh_token_validity_days` defaults to 30 in
# infra/modules/cognito/variables.tf), sat in script-readable Web Storage on the
# origin that renders every screen. Any script executing on this origin could
# read all four, and a renewal token read once mints one-hour bearers for a month
# from anywhere. Holding nothing REMOVES that exposure instead of accepting it,
# which is a different kind of answer from narrowing where the credential is kept.
#
# Lifetime: the session lasts as long as the page. It survives an in-tab route
# change, because routing is client-side and the module is not re-evaluated, and
# it does NOT survive a reload, a restored tab or a second tab -- each of those
# loads the bundle afresh and the operator signs on again. It cannot outlive the
# page and cannot be shared with another tab, because there is nowhere for it to
# outlive the page in.
#
# Trade-offs: the cost is that a reload ends the session, which the 3270 workflow
# this replaces did not do -- a terminal session survived far more than a refresh.
# That cost is accepted deliberately and is recorded as divergence
# D-SESSION-NOT-PERSISTED in
# docs/architecture/cobol-to-service-traceability.md, where the alternatives are
# argued in full. In outline: an HttpOnly cookie would be unreadable by script
# and would be the right answer for a same-origin deployment, but the SPA is
# served by CloudFront and the API by API Gateway (see
# infra/modules/cloudfront-spa, which declares one S3 origin and no API
# behaviour), so the cookie would need SameSite=None -- readmitting cross-site
# requests and letting any page trigger a rotation that invalidates the
# legitimate tab's token. A server-held session is foreclosed by the
# architecture itself: the services are stateless by requirement and there is no
# cache tier to hold one in.
#
# Assumptions: the earlier reasoning that a content-security policy admitting no
# third-party script left "no supply-chain path to a reader" was FALSE, and it is
# worth stating plainly because it is a tempting argument. Third-party code in
# this application is BUNDLED -- antd, axios, react and their dependencies are
# compiled into the bundle the policy serves from 'self' -- so a compromised
# dependency executes as first-party script and the policy never sees a
# third-party origin to refuse. A policy is a defence against loading foreign
# code, not against foreign code already inside the bundle.
#
# Discarded on: an explicit sign-out, a renewal the pool refuses, and any 401
# answered to a request that CARRIED a bearer. All three run one `discardSession`
# path that drops the whole record at once, so no signed claim can outlive the
# credential it accompanied -- a partial clear is not expressible, because there
# is one value rather than four. A 401 from the sign-on request itself does NOT
# discard anything, because that status means a rejected password rather than an
# expired session. A 403 does not either: it is a valid token refused one route,
# and signing the operator out would invite them to fix something that is not
# broken.
#
# Assumptions: an explicit sign-out additionally REVOKES the renewal token at the
# provider, so the session ends everywhere rather than only in this page. The
# local discard happens first and unconditionally: a refused or unreachable
# revocation must not leave a signed-out operator signed on.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: an earlier revision of this file declared four
# VITE_COGNITO_* / VITE_AWS_REGION names on the reasoning that a browser SPA is
# a public OAuth client which discovers the pool and begins its own sign-in
# flow. That is a coherent design -- authorization code with PKCE against a
# hosted sign-in page -- and it is NOT the design this migration implements, so
# the two halves contradicted each other: the SPA surface described a public
# client with no secret and no hosted domain, while the auth service was
# configured as a CONFIDENTIAL client with a secret, and the provider's token
# endpoint implements no password grant that either could have used. Two
# incompatible flows half-configured is worse than either alone, because each
# half reads as correct beside its own neighbours and the contradiction is only
# visible from a document that sees both.
#
# The flow that IS implemented, stated once here so the absence above reads as a
# decision: the SPA renders the baseline's own sign-on screen, transcribed from
# app/bms/COSGN00.bms, and the auth service authenticates the submitted
# credential against the user pool server-side through the provider's
# authentication API, computing its SECRET_HASH from a client secret that never
# leaves the server. Delegating instead to a Cognito-hosted page was evaluated
# and rejected because it would strand that screen entirely, along with the
# verbatim baseline replies the migration is required to preserve, which are
# values ui/src/messages/messages.ts already carries character for character.
# Assumptions: ⚠️ TWO of those replies are actually reachable, not three. The
# catalog carries all three sentences from app/cpy/CSMSG01Y.cpy, but the auth
# service answers a wrong password AND an unknown identifier with the same
# 'Wrong Password. Try again ...' sentence so that neither reply reveals whether
# an operator exists; 'User not found. Try again ...' is therefore never emitted.
# That is registered as divergence D-SIGNON-EXISTENCE-UNIFORM. The other two --
# 'Wrong Password. Try again ...' and 'Unable to verify the User ...' -- are
# rendered verbatim, and the catalog keeps the unreachable third because it is
# baseline text and the register is where its absence is explained.
#
# Assumptions: anything supplied for a name in this file is compiled into the
# public bundle and is readable by anyone who loads the page. That is why the
# absence is structural rather than stylistic -- there is no value the SPA could
# hold that would let it authenticate directly without also publishing the means
# to impersonate it -- and it is why no VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_SECRET may ever be
# added here under any circumstances.
#
# Refactoring Rationale: privilege in the migrated application comes from a
# SIGNED `cognito:groups` claim -- `carddemo-admin` or `carddemo-user` -- and
# that is the most consequential security correction in the online migration.
# The baseline carried privilege in `CDEMO-USER-TYPE PIC X(01)`, with
# `88 CDEMO-USRTYP-ADMIN VALUE 'A'` and `88 CDEMO-USRTYP-USER VALUE 'U'`
# (app/cpy/COCOM01Y.cpy L26-L28), inside the CICS communication area the
# terminal echoed back between pseudo-conversational turns -- storage the client
# itself returned, so a client could in principle assert its own privilege
# level. A signed claim cannot be asserted by the client. Consequently NO
# variable in this file configures a user type, a group or an administrative
# flag: admin gating reads the claim and nothing else, and introducing such a
# name here would reintroduce exactly the weakness this design removes. The SPA
# only DECODES the claim to choose which route to show; every service revalidates
# the token and re-derives the authority for itself, so a tampered local copy
# changes what the browser draws and nothing about what it is allowed to do.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cross-cutting request behaviour
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Names the HTTP header the axios interceptor stamps with a per-request
# correlation identifier, so one action in the browser can be followed across
# every service it fans out to.
# Assumptions: the value is dictated by the services rather than chosen here --
# it must match the header the server-side CorrelationIdFilter reads. A mismatch
# fails SILENTLY: no request errors and nothing reports anything wrong; the
# identifier is simply absent from the server's logging context and traces
# quietly stop joining up, which is the worst failure mode available because
# nothing signals it.
# Assumptions: this name is read through import.meta.env, which Vite inlines at
# BUILD time, and the server's name is a public static final literal that is
# deliberately not configurable at all. So a rename is a coordinated change on
# both sides -- a source edit and a redeploy on the service, and a rebuilt SPA
# bundle here -- and setting this variable does not avoid either. Trade-offs:
# what it buys is that the browser half is a build setting rather than an edit to
# ui/src/api/client.ts, and that a mistyped name is refused at startup as a
# non-HTTP-token instead of joining the silent mismatch described above; leave it
# blank unless the service constant has actually been changed.
VITE_CORRELATION_ID_HEADER=

# Milliseconds after which the axios instance abandons a request, mirroring the
# explicit connect and read timeouts the services apply to their own synchronous
# calls so that a stalled dependency surfaces as a bounded error rather than a
# screen that hangs indefinitely.
# Assumptions: this is an operational tuning decision taken per environment,
# not a provisioned coordinate, so it is the one name here that no Terraform
# output supplies -- which is also what makes the trade-off below a real choice.
# Trade-offs: this is the one entry that could defensibly carry a value, since a
# timeout is a behavioural default and discloses nothing environment-specific in
# the way a hostname or an identifier does. It is left empty anyway, to keep the
# file's rule absolute: one populated entry invites the next, and a single grep
# asserting that NO right-hand side is populated is a stronger guard than one
# carrying a documented exception a reader has to evaluate. The cost is that
# ui/src/api/client.ts must supply its own fallback, which is where a
# behavioural default belongs in any case.
VITE_API_TIMEOUT_MS=


# =============================================================================
# Usage
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   cp ui/.env.example ui/.env
#
# Assumptions: the root .gitignore ignores `.env`, `.env.local` and
# `.env.*.local` while negating `!.env.example` and `!**/.env.example`, so the
# working copy stays untracked while this template stays tracked. That asymmetry
# is deliberate: documenting the configuration contract is a deliverable of the
# migration, and the filled-in copy never is.
# =============================================================================
